*Abstract Class
An abstract class is a class that:

Cannot be instantiated (you cannot create its object)
Can have abstract methods (methods without body)
Can also have normal methods, variables, and constructors

 It works as a blueprint for other classes.

Abstract Method

An abstract method:
Uses the abstract keyword
Has no method body
Must be implemented by the child class

abstract void show();

Syntax of Abstract Class
abstract class Animal {

    abstract void sound();   // abstract method

    void sleep() {            // normal method
        System.out.println("Animal is sleeping");
    }
}

Child Class Implementation
class Dog extends Animal {

    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Dog barks");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Dog d = new Dog();
        d.sound();
        d.sleep();
    }
}

Important Points (Exam Use)

You cannot create an object of an abstract class
Animal a = new Animal(); // Error


An abstract class can contain:

abstract methods
non-abstract methods
variables

constructors

If a child class does not implement all abstract methods,
the child class must also be declared abstract

abstract and final cannot be used together

Abstract Class vs Interface (Short)
Abstract Class	Interface
Methods with body allowed	Methods are abstract by default
Instance variables allowed	Variables are public static final
Constructor allowed	Constructor not allowed
Multiple inheritance No	Multiple inheritance Yes

When to Use Abstract Class?

When you want a base class
When some methods should have implementation and some should be forced on child classes